    {
      "title": "BMW Industries starts colour coated steel production to cater demand",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/companies/news/bmw-industries-starts-colour-coated-steel-production-to-cater-demand-126101100157_1.html",
      "description": "BMW Industries has begun manufacturing colour-coated steel to meet growing domestic demand from key sectors such as infrastructure, auto and consumer goods, the company's Managing Director Harsh Bansal said.\nThe new colour-coated steel line, commissioned on Monday, is part of the company's ongoing Rs 800-crore expansion plan at Bokaro in Jharkhand, aimed at offering special steel products, Bansal said.\nKolkata-based BMW Industries has until now manufactured steel coils, sheets, pipes and TMT rebars.\n\"We have commenced the first phase of our greenfield downstream steel complex with the colour coating line. With this, we have joined the select group of producers in India. The line has been set up as part of the government's PLI Scheme for Speciality Steel,\" he said.\nLaunched in 2021 by the steel ministry with an outlay of Rs 6,322 crore, the first round of the PLI Scheme aims to promote the manufacturing of value-added steel grades in the country and help the Indian steel industry ..",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-11T05:48:11"
    },
    {
      "title": "Govt releases draft National Steel Policy 2047, targets 604 MT capacity",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/industry/news/govt-releases-draft-national-steel-policy-2047-targets-604-mt-capacity-126101100130_1.html",
      "description": "The government has released a new steel policy to boost India's crude steel capacity to 604 million tonnes by 2047, enhance industry competitiveness and address key import challenges.\nAccording to the draft National Steel Policy 2047, finished steel demand will rise to 505 MT by 2047, per capita consumption to 302 kg and steel exports to 45 MT.\nRaw materials account for around 65-70 per cent of steelmaking costs.\nWhile India is largely self-sufficient in iron ore, it imports nearly 92 per cent of its coking coal requirement.\nThe policy seeks to reduce import dependence through domestic mining, washery expansion, asset acquisition, long-term supply arrangements, and AI-based optimisation of coal blends.\nOther raw materials important for the steel sector, like Manganese, Limestone etc., will also remain in policy focus to ensure long-term resource sustainability.\nThe secondary steel sector, comprising around 2,550 units and nearly 47 per cent of crude steel production capacity, wil",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-11T05:12:51"
    }
  ]
